WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:03.448
This is research from
the Federal Modernization

3
00:00:03.448 --> 00:00:06.462
Workbook's Research section,
and it's still under review.

4
00:00:06.462 --> 00:00:10.941
It's a companion to Book 08 of the
workbook, on DNS, DHCP and IPAM,

5
00:00:10.941 --> 00:00:15.935
and it asks one question: if an agency
is going to buy or build a platform

6
00:00:15.935 --> 00:00:21.188
for its name and address layer, what
does that platform actually have to do?

7
00:00:21.188 --> 00:00:23.963
We'll cover why that layer
is now a control plane,

8
00:00:23.963 --> 00:00:28.232
what a platform has to deliver and what
to check before buying, who runs it,

9
00:00:28.232 --> 00:00:32.288
including how divisions can manage
their own piece, and how to decide.

10
00:00:32.288 --> 00:00:36.611
Vendors appear only as examples,
nothing here ranks a product,

11
00:00:36.611 --> 00:00:42.730
and every claim comes from Book 08 or from
primary sources checked in October 2026.

12
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:48.530
Here's why this layer deserves attention.

13
00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.968
Almost every connection
starts with a DNS query,

14
00:00:51.968 --> 00:00:57.468
and in a hybrid multi-cloud agency the
answer decides far more than an address.

15
00:00:57.468 --> 00:01:01.444
NIST's 2026 DNS deployment
guide puts it plainly:

16
00:01:01.444 --> 00:01:06.117
DNS has become more than a
service; it's a security control.

17
00:01:06.117 --> 00:01:08.740
Yet most agencies run it across as many as

18
00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.593
five separate platforms
that share no state.

19
00:01:11.593 --> 00:01:16.738
The result is split-brain DNS, the same
name giving different answers depending

20
00:01:16.738 --> 00:01:21.806
on where you ask, and it fails quietly,
as certificate errors and access denials.

21
00:01:21.806 --> 00:01:25.917
IPAM and DHCP are the address
half of the same problem.

22
00:01:25.917 --> 00:01:29.908
The book's answer is one
authoritative DDI policy plane,

23
00:01:29.908 --> 00:01:31.904
a requirement, not a product.

24
00:01:31.904 --> 00:01:33.960
So what do the rules ask for?

25
00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:42.010
Before any procurement, one thing
to know: no memo, directive,

26
00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:46.047
executive order or NIST
publication names a DDI vendor,

27
00:01:46.047 --> 00:01:50.260
or even requires a product
category called a DDI platform.

28
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.072
They require capabilities.

29
00:01:52.072 --> 00:01:54.960
Signed answers and validating resolvers.

30
00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.498
Encrypted DNS wherever it's
technically supported.

31
00:01:58.498 --> 00:02:03.260
Protective DNS for all egress
resolution at civilian agencies.

32
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:06.482
Regular discovery of
agency address space and

33
00:02:06.482 --> 00:02:09.622
attestation of public
addresses and domains.

34
00:02:09.622 --> 00:02:15.398
On logging, be precise: the current memo
sets retention but doesn't mention DNS,

35
00:02:15.398 --> 00:02:21.534
so DNS logging rests on guidance mapped to
that baseline, not a standalone mandate.

36
00:02:21.534 --> 00:02:24.142
And NIST's DNS guide is guidance too,

37
00:02:24.142 --> 00:02:28.097
with two of its three authors
working for a DDI vendor.

38
00:02:28.097 --> 00:02:30.500
Now the capabilities themselves.

39
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:39.025
There are seven capabilities, and the
first two are about authority and trust.

40
00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:44.125
First, authoritative DNS across every
environment without split-brain.

41
00:02:44.125 --> 00:02:46.111
The platform holds each zone,

42
00:02:46.111 --> 00:02:50.250
forwarding rule and policy once,
and pushes it to every cloud.

43
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:54.163
Book 08 calls that overlay
management, not replacement.

44
00:02:54.163 --> 00:02:57.256
When you evaluate a product,
ask whether each cloud

45
00:02:57.256 --> 00:03:01.087
integration actually writes to
the cloud, or only reads from it.

46
00:03:01.087 --> 00:03:06.619
Second, DNSSEC with central key custody:
sign external zones always, validate

47
00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:12.237
on every agency resolver, and keep rollover
automated and audited in one place.

48
00:03:12.237 --> 00:03:17.202
The clouds don't fill this gap, and
check the defaults: Windows Server's

49
00:03:17.202 --> 00:03:22.601
default signing key is below NIST's floor,
and NIOS defaults to RSA with SHA-1.

50
00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.490
Next, security.

51
00:03:28.240 --> 00:03:33.328
The third capability is DNS security,
and it's two layers in series.

52
00:03:33.328 --> 00:03:37.972
The agency's own resolvers apply the
agency's policy and log every hit,

53
00:03:37.972 --> 00:03:42.941
and then all outbound queries go
upstream through CISA's Protective DNS.

54
00:03:42.941 --> 00:03:45.527
The order matters more
than having the pieces.

55
00:03:45.527 --> 00:03:50.455
Book 08's Decision 2 is that the agency
resolver is always the first hop,

56
00:03:50.455 --> 00:03:54.363
so a SASE provider's DNS
service is a forwarding target,

57
00:03:54.363 --> 00:03:57.677
never the place the branch
sends queries first.

58
00:03:57.677 --> 00:04:02.102
Get the order wrong and you quietly
lose the agency's own policy,

59
00:04:02.102 --> 00:04:03.803
validation and logging.

60
00:04:03.803 --> 00:04:07.943
Encrypted DNS completes the
picture: the platform terminates

61
00:04:07.943 --> 00:04:11.527
it on its resolvers, and
clients mustn't route around it.

62
00:04:11.527 --> 00:04:13.300
Next, addresses.

63
00:04:17.050 --> 00:04:20.100
The fourth and fifth capabilities
are about addresses.

64
00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:24.003
Left alone, each cloud's address
manager allocates on its own,

65
00:04:24.003 --> 00:04:26.813
which recreates split-brain for addresses.

66
00:04:26.813 --> 00:04:30.112
So the book's rule is simple:
every block is reserved in

67
00:04:30.112 --> 00:04:33.787
the enterprise IPAM before
any cloud network is created.

68
00:04:33.787 --> 00:04:36.688
Discovery is where IPAM
meets the directives,

69
00:04:36.688 --> 00:04:39.588
because you can't scan
space you haven't recorded.

70
00:04:39.588 --> 00:04:43.937
And IPv4 and IPv6 have to be
planned in the same tool.

71
00:04:43.937 --> 00:04:46.813
DHCP has to stay up and stay honest.

72
00:04:46.813 --> 00:04:50.920
Failover partners share the
same DNS update credentials,

73
00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.087
or a takeover leaves
names nobody can update.

74
00:04:54.087 --> 00:04:59.058
And in the two products checked,
failover is documented for IPv4 only,

75
00:04:59.058 --> 00:05:01.762
which matters under the IPv6 targets.

76
00:05:01.762 --> 00:05:04.270
Then, automation and logging.

77
00:05:08.020 --> 00:05:11.132
The last two capabilities
are automation and logging.

78
00:05:11.132 --> 00:05:16.521
No rule requires an API, but Book
08's operating model depends on one.

79
00:05:16.521 --> 00:05:20.072
Every object should be reachable
through a documented API,

80
00:05:20.072 --> 00:05:24.566
so infrastructure as code, ticketing
and pipelines all go through the same

81
00:05:24.566 --> 00:05:29.495
authority and audit trail, with service
accounts held to the same scopes as people.

82
00:05:29.495 --> 00:05:32.482
Logging to the SIEM has to
meet the retention rule:

83
00:05:32.482 --> 00:05:35.320
six months searchable
and a year retrievable.

84
00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:37.733
Capacity is where designs fail.

85
00:05:37.733 --> 00:05:42.892
Full query logging costs performance,
reporting appliances can lock after repeated

86
00:05:42.892 --> 00:05:47.470
ingest overruns, and some log streams
drop records when the receiver is down.

87
00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:50.983
So size for peak, and test
with the SIEM unreachable.

88
00:05:50.983 --> 00:05:52.450
Next, buying.

89
00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.650
Before buying anything,

90
00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:01.275
check the evidence an assessor
will ask for, not the brochure.

91
00:06:01.275 --> 00:06:05.637
Start with FedRAMP, and read a
listing's scope, not just its badge.

92
00:06:05.637 --> 00:06:10.326
It covers a specific cloud offering,
not the software or appliances the

93
00:06:10.326 --> 00:06:14.775
agency runs itself, and Moderate
doesn't cover High or DoD workloads.

94
00:06:14.775 --> 00:06:19.854
Then ask about the STIG, and ask for
an active FIPS 140-3 certificate,

95
00:06:19.854 --> 00:06:22.163
checking its status yourself.

96
00:06:22.163 --> 00:06:28.537
FIPS 140-2 certificates moved to NIST's
historical list on September 21,

97
00:06:28.537 --> 00:06:31.887
2026, so a new deployment can't cite one.

98
00:06:31.887 --> 00:06:36.475
Ask how the vendor tracks the
open-source DNS code underneath.

99
00:06:36.475 --> 00:06:41.137
And understand what's metered, then
model the cost over three to five years.

100
00:06:41.137 --> 00:06:43.180
Here's what those checks returned.

101
00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:52.880
Here's what those checks returned for
four DDI products on October 22, 2026.

102
00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.930
It's a snapshot, not a ranking,
so confirm on the day you decide.

103
00:06:56.930 --> 00:07:01.347
Only Infoblox Government Cloud has
a FedRAMP listing, at Moderate,

104
00:07:01.347 --> 00:07:04.880
and it covers the SaaS, not
the on-premises appliances.

105
00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:08.005
The others run inside the
agency's own boundary.

106
00:07:08.005 --> 00:07:10.893
Only Infoblox has vendor-specific STIGs.

107
00:07:10.893 --> 00:07:14.756
Only BlueCat holds an active
FIPS 140-3 certificate,

108
00:07:14.756 --> 00:07:19.429
while Infoblox's newer modules
are still in NIST's review queue.

109
00:07:19.429 --> 00:07:22.964
None of the four has a
current NIAP certification,

110
00:07:22.964 --> 00:07:25.292
and none is on the latest CDM list.

111
00:07:25.292 --> 00:07:27.872
So every candidate has a gap to close,

112
00:07:27.872 --> 00:07:31.568
in the contract or in a plan
of action and milestones.

113
00:07:31.568 --> 00:07:33.120
Now, who runs it.

114
00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:42.152
Now the question many agencies care about
most: can divisions run their own DNS,

115
00:07:42.152 --> 00:07:45.808
DHCP and IPAM without
breaking central governance?

116
00:07:45.808 --> 00:07:49.795
Book 08 says yes, if the platform
supports the right model.

117
00:07:49.795 --> 00:07:55.051
Corporate administrators see everything,
and each division gets a fenced scope of

118
00:07:55.051 --> 00:07:57.869
its own zones, address
blocks and DHCP scopes,

119
00:07:57.869 --> 00:08:00.383
with everything else denied by default.

120
00:08:00.383 --> 00:08:04.743
In Infoblox, for example, one setting
stops a division with access to a

121
00:08:04.743 --> 00:08:09.257
shared zone from pointing a name into
another division's address space.

122
00:08:09.257 --> 00:08:12.593
Only central superusers
create groups and roles,

123
00:08:12.593 --> 00:08:15.196
so a division can't widen its own rights.

124
00:08:15.196 --> 00:08:19.096
Administrators sign in with
PIV or CAC, or single sign-on,

125
00:08:19.096 --> 00:08:21.895
and you keep a local break-glass account.

126
00:08:21.895 --> 00:08:24.140
So how are divisions kept apart?

127
00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:30.252
Three constructs keep divisions apart.

128
00:08:30.252 --> 00:08:32.628
First, separate address spaces.

129
00:08:32.628 --> 00:08:37.665
When a lab's private range overlaps
headquarters and renumbering isn't practical,

130
00:08:37.665 --> 00:08:42.777
the platform needs a separate routing domain
so the same addresses can exist twice,

131
00:08:42.777 --> 00:08:45.815
and that division then
needs its own DHCP servers.

132
00:08:45.815 --> 00:08:50.578
Second, DNS views, which give
different answers to different clients.

133
00:08:50.578 --> 00:08:53.415
The first match wins,
so the order matters.

134
00:08:53.415 --> 00:08:57.690
Third, tags for ownership,
like division or cost center.

135
00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:01.573
They drive reports, not permissions,
and a required tag can be

136
00:09:01.573 --> 00:09:05.378
skipped through the API, so the
automation has to enforce it.

137
00:09:05.378 --> 00:09:09.449
One trap to watch: report data
may ignore object permissions,

138
00:09:09.449 --> 00:09:11.728
so share dashboards per division.

139
00:09:11.728 --> 00:09:13.910
Next, change control.

140
00:09:17.660 --> 00:09:22.610
Delegation needs change control, and the
useful pattern is an approval workflow.

141
00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:26.865
A division submits a change with a ticket
number, a central group approves it,

142
00:09:26.865 --> 00:09:29.904
and the task runs with the
submitter's own permissions,

143
00:09:29.904 --> 00:09:33.010
so approval can't grant rights
the division doesn't have.

144
00:09:33.010 --> 00:09:36.185
The weakness of any workflow
is what goes around it.

145
00:09:36.185 --> 00:09:41.042
In NIOS, for example, CSV imports
and service restarts skip approval,

146
00:09:41.042 --> 00:09:44.685
so remove import rights
from every division group.

147
00:09:44.685 --> 00:09:49.347
And a zone delegated to a division's own
servers leaves the platform entirely.

148
00:09:49.347 --> 00:09:55.067
So decide what stays central in every
pattern: superusers, roles, approvals,

149
00:09:55.067 --> 00:10:01.248
sign-in policy, DNSSEC keys, security policy,
top-level address blocks, and logging.

150
00:10:01.248 --> 00:10:03.730
Those pieces make four patterns.

151
00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:10.348
Those pieces make four
delegation patterns,

152
00:10:10.348 --> 00:10:14.068
each with a cost, and they apply
to any comparable platform.

153
00:10:14.068 --> 00:10:18.215
Pattern A is one central platform
with delegated admin groups:

154
00:10:18.215 --> 00:10:22.043
one database, one audit log,
but a risk of permission sprawl.

155
00:10:22.043 --> 00:10:25.672
Pattern B adds division-owned
servers inside the platform,

156
00:10:25.672 --> 00:10:29.968
for local resilience, while signed
zones still keep a central primary.

157
00:10:29.968 --> 00:10:33.156
Pattern C gives a division
its own network view,

158
00:10:33.156 --> 00:10:36.343
the only way to hold
overlapping address space.

159
00:10:36.343 --> 00:10:41.748
And pattern D delegates a zone to servers
the division runs itself: full autonomy,

160
00:10:41.748 --> 00:10:46.831
but central loses the audit trail, policy
coverage and signing for that zone.

161
00:10:46.831 --> 00:10:50.950
The patterns combine, and the
worked example uses all four.

162
00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:58.726
Here's an illustrative design,
not a validated configuration,

163
00:10:58.726 --> 00:11:00.937
for an agency with four divisions.

164
00:11:00.937 --> 00:11:03.958
Headquarters is the
central operations team,

165
00:11:03.958 --> 00:11:08.887
holding its own block and zone under
pattern A, plus the central superusers.

166
00:11:08.887 --> 00:11:13.674
A law-enforcement bureau needs confidentiality
from the other divisions, so it

167
00:11:13.674 --> 00:11:18.388
gets its own block, zone and servers, and
its data stays out of shared dashboards.

168
00:11:18.388 --> 00:11:23.817
A regional office still runs Windows DNS
and DHCP, which the platform manages,

169
00:11:23.817 --> 00:11:27.888
with the region's group given
rights on those servers only.

170
00:11:27.888 --> 00:11:32.683
And a research lab that numbered itself
into an overlapping range gets its

171
00:11:32.683 --> 00:11:37.325
own network view and DHCP pair, with
its zone delegated to its own servers.

172
00:11:37.325 --> 00:11:39.760
Then the question is who decides.

173
00:11:43.510 --> 00:11:44.810
Who decides all this?

174
00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:50.985
Book 08's Chapter 09 gives one decider per
row, and separates deciding from doing.

175
00:11:50.985 --> 00:11:55.260
The sponsor and CIO choose the
platform and how divisions are scoped.

176
00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:58.670
The authorizing official decides
whether a cloud-delivered

177
00:11:58.670 --> 00:12:02.547
management plane is authorized, and
accepts the standing exceptions.

178
00:12:02.547 --> 00:12:06.384
The CISO and ISSO set
the control requirements.

179
00:12:06.384 --> 00:12:10.589
And the DNS and DDI team owns the
namespace and the address plan,

180
00:12:10.589 --> 00:12:12.853
and approves high-impact changes,

181
00:12:12.853 --> 00:12:17.947
while routine record changes inside a
division's own space are pre-approved.

182
00:12:17.947 --> 00:12:22.564
Because that team holds the most powerful
roles, the book scopes its automation,

183
00:12:22.564 --> 00:12:25.361
adds a second approver
for the riskiest changes,

184
00:12:25.361 --> 00:12:28.880
and keeps its privileged
roles eligible, not standing.

185
00:12:32.630 --> 00:12:35.617
To wrap up, the decisions and next steps.

186
00:12:35.617 --> 00:12:41.510
The sponsor and CIO authorize one
authoritative DDI policy plane, and the DNS

187
00:12:41.510 --> 00:12:47.218
and DDI team settles Book 08's five
configuration decisions within 90 days.

188
00:12:47.218 --> 00:12:49.750
Write the requirement as capabilities,

189
00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:53.204
each mapped to its rule,
because no rule names a product.

190
00:12:53.204 --> 00:12:58.480
Ask every candidate for the same evidence
pack, and record every gap with an owner.

191
00:12:58.480 --> 00:13:01.779
Pick a delegation pattern
per division, and pilot it,

192
00:13:01.779 --> 00:13:05.155
ideally with one that has
overlapping address space.

193
00:13:05.155 --> 00:13:08.293
And size logging for
retention, tested at peak.

194
00:13:08.293 --> 00:13:12.453
The next step needs no award:
list every DNS, DHCP and IPAM

195
00:13:12.453 --> 00:13:17.567
requirement your systems inherit,
and the evidence that would prove it.

196
00:13:17.567 --> 00:13:18.400
Thank you.
